# Mathematics still to be completed
#
# Section I — finite_set_minus_cardinality_background
# Prove the cardinality formula for removing one finite set from another.
#
# Section I — finite_set_zero_empty_background
# Prove that a finite set of cardinality zero is empty.
#
# Section II — gcd_Z_partial_bezout
# The ordinary gcd Bezout coefficients are constructed. It remains to prove
# explicitly that the same coefficients satisfy Bezout's identity for the
# gcd restricted to nonzero input pairs.
#
# Section IV — prime_divides_some_factor_background
# Prove that a prime dividing a finite product divides at least one factor.
#
# Section IV — prime_factorization_background
# Prove the finite prime factorization statement retained from the source.
#
# Section IV — exact_prime_power_background
# Prove existence and uniqueness of the largest exponent whose prime power
# divides a given integer greater than one.
#
# Section IV — primes_unbounded_background
# Complete Euclid's construction proving that primes exceed every bound.
#
# Section V — congruence_classes_additive_group_background
# Construct the finite residue-class quotient and prove its additive group
# laws.
#
# Section VI — euler_phi_exists_background
# Construct the finite set of reduced residue representatives and prove that
# its cardinality is uniquely determined.
#
# Section VI — residue_unitary_ring_background
# Construct the residue-class ring with identity and prove its ring laws.
#
# Section VI — linear_congruence_solution_background
# Prove the full solution-family description for a linear congruence.
#
# Section VI — euler_phi_spec_value_background
# Prove that the selected Euler phi value has the stated representative-count
# characterization.
#
# Section VI — euler_phi_prime_background
# Prove that Euler phi of a prime p is p - 1.
#
# Section VI — residue_field_prime_background
# Prove that residue classes modulo a prime form a field.
#
# Section VI — residue_field_only_prime_background
# Prove conversely that a residue-class field has prime modulus.
#
# Section VI — residue_field_iff_prime_background
# Combine the two directions into the field-if-and-only-if-prime statement.
#
# Section VII — generated_elements_power_background
# Prove that every element of a singly generated subgroup is a power of its
# generator.
#
# Section VII — finite_cyclic_power_classes_background
# Prove the finite cyclic power-class description and its counting statement.
#
# Section VIII — element_order_exists_background
# Prove existence and uniqueness of the order of an element in a finite group.
#
# Section VIII — subgroup_order_divides_group_order
# Prove the subgroup-order divisibility theorem by finite coset counting.
#
# Section VIII — euler_theorem_background
# Derive Euler's theorem from the finite group of reduced residue classes.
#
# Section IX — polynomial_gcd_generator_background
# Construct a greatest common divisor generator for a polynomial family.
#
# Section IX — polynomial_euclidean_data
# Construct the generic polynomial carrier, finite-support coefficient
# operations, degree, and Euclidean division data over a coefficient field.
#
# Section IX — polynomial_division_background
# Prove polynomial division with remainder over the retained coefficient
# setting.
#
# Section IX — polynomial_root_bound_background
# Prove that a nonzero polynomial has no more roots than its degree.
#
# Section X — index_mod_exists_background
# Prove existence and uniqueness of the index of a reduced residue with
# respect to a primitive root.
#
# Section X — finite_subgroup_generator_background
# Prove that the retained finite multiplicative subgroup is cyclic.
#
# Section X — prime_primitive_root_background
# Prove existence of a primitive root modulo a prime.
#
# Section XI — power_congruence_solution_background
# Prove the forward finite-field criterion for a prime-modulus power
# congruence.
#
# Section XI — power_congruence_reduced_background
# Prove the converse finite-field criterion for that power congruence.
#
# Section XI — euler_criterion_background
# Prove Euler's criterion for quadratic residues modulo an odd prime.
#
# Section XII — legendre_symbol_exists_background
# Prove that the zero, residue, and nonresidue cases select a unique Legendre
# symbol value.
#
# Section XII — gauss_lemma_background
# Prove Gauss's lemma by counting the excess representatives.
#
# Section XII — quadratic_reciprocity_background
# Prove the quadratic reciprocity law.
#
# Section XIII — gaussian_division_background
# Prove Euclidean division with norm-decreasing remainder for Gaussian
# integers.
#
# Section XIII — gaussian_prime_factor_background
# Prove the Gaussian-prime factorization statement.
#
# Section XIII — rational_prime_split_background
# Prove the rational-prime splitting and two-squares statement.
